  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nModel runs continue to show heavy rainfall the latter part of this \nweek and into the weekend as a cold front approaches S TX on Friday \nand possibly stalls Friday night into Saturday along the coast. \nThere remains a lot of uncertainty with regard to the cold front's \ntiming, strength, and if it will stall across S TX.\n\nAhead of this rain event, heat remains a concern through Wednesday \nwith above normal temperatures continuing under a ridge of high \npressure. The ridge axis is forecast to slowly move east across the \narea through the day. Warm air advection and higher RH values may \nlead to heat indices around 110 for more than a couple of hours \nacross the eastern 2/3rd of S TX  Wednesday afternoon, which may \nwarrant a Heat Advisory. Will continue to monitor trends. Slightly \ncooler temperatures are expected beginning Thursday with increased \nclouds/rain, then even cooler behind a cold front by the end of the \nweek with highs in the 80s area-wide and lows in the upper 60s to \n70s.\n\nA long wave mid/upper level trough is progged to deepen as it tracks \neast-southeast across the western U.S. and approaches West Texas by \nmid week. This will strengthen the low level winds across S TX and \nusher deeper moisture across the area from the gulf in the low and \nmid levels. The low will also tap into mid/upper level moisture from \nthe remnants of Hurricane Polo in the Pacific over the next couple \nof days. PWATs are progged to increase to 2.2-2.4 inches beginning \nWednesday and as high as 2.5 inches Thursday. This deep moisture \ncombined with embedded short waves tracking northeast ahead of the \nlong wave trough, will lead to a 20-35% chance of showers and \nthunderstorms across mainly the Victoria Crossroads on Wednesday. \nWidespread convection is expected across S TX by Thursday as the \nupper trough approaches from the west and a weak prefrontal surface \ntrough develops over S TX. \n\nA cold front associated with the upper trough is forecast to move \ninto S TX Friday and possibly stall somewhere across S TX Friday \nnight into Saturday morning. If the cold front stalls anywhere in S \nTX, that will be a focus for the heaviest rainfall and possible \nflash flooding. There remains uncertainty with the cold front's \ntiming, strength and how far south it progresses.  For now, it looks \nlike it will stall Friday night along the coast, then push off the \ncoast by Sunday with winds strengthening from the north and cooler \ntemperatures advecting into S TX. Medium rain chances continue \nthrough the weekend as the upper low slowly tracks east across TX. \n\nConfidence is moderate to high with respect to increasing rain due \nto the upper low and abundant deep moisture. However, confidence \nwith respect to the cold front remains low as model solutions \ncontinue to differ from run to run on timing and just how far south \nthe front goes before stalling.",
